{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent upward bias in true outcomes vs model from ~day 10 onward: add a positive drift that increases with horizon (small/near-zero for days 1–5, moderate for days 6–15, large for days 16–30).",
    "Trend/lag error: model reacts too slowly to regime shifts (notably the late-horizon rally to high-60s). When the last 5–10 days of history show stabilization after a drawdown, tilt the whole path upward rather than mean-reverting.",
    "Underestimation of upside tails: during rebound phases the model caps prices around mid-60s while realized reaches ~68–70. Apply an upside expansion (raise peaks) when recent volatility is elevated and direction turns positive.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: early horizon (days 1–5) is reasonably calibrated; mid horizon (days 6–15) slightly low; long horizon (days 16–30) materially low. Use piecewise additive corrections by horizon bucket.",
    "Mean-reversion too strong: model pulls forecasts back toward ~63–65 even when the realized path trends. Reduce mean-reversion strength when recent momentum (e.g., 3–7 day return) is positive.",
    "After local dips, realized rebounds more than forecast: if the current spot is near a 20–30 day low, add a rebound premium that grows with horizon (the deeper the drawdown, the larger the premium)."
  ]
}
